WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
Spots of light drive the retina well.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.480
In cat visual cortex, the same spots often did little.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.560
A bar at the right angle drove some cells hard.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.500
Why would a bar win?

5
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:16.453
Start in the retina.

6
00:00:17.453 --> 00:00:21.333
Every visual neuron watches one patch of the world, its receptive field.

7
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:24.383
Take an ON-centre ganglion cell.

8
00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:27.363
Light on its small round centre excites it.

9
00:00:28.063 --> 00:00:30.783
Light on the ring around it, the surround, inhibits it.

10
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:34.127
Shine a small spot that just fills the centre.

11
00:00:34.127 --> 00:00:37.887
It lands only on plus, so the cell fires faster.

12
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:41.627
Now light the whole patch, centre and ring together.

13
00:00:42.547 --> 00:00:43.547
What will the cell do?

14
00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:55.057
Very little. The centre adds plus, the ring adds minus, and the two largely cancel.

15
00:00:56.217 --> 00:01:00.997
Light the ring alone, and the sum goes negative, so firing drops below its

16
00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:01.997
spontaneous rate.

17
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.850
When that light goes off, the cell gives a brief burst.

18
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.590
That burst answers a change in light, which the sum alone does not capture.

19
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:14.273
So here is the rule for cells like this one.

20
00:01:15.073 --> 00:01:16.133
Add up the light in the field.

21
00:01:17.153 --> 00:01:18.733
Light on an ON region counts as plus.

22
00:01:19.693 --> 00:01:21.453
Light on an OFF region counts as minus.

23
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.567
A bigger sum means stronger drive.

24
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.927
A sum near zero leaves the spontaneous rate.

25
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:33.207
A positive sum can raise firing, and a negative sum lowers it, down to zero.

26
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.450
Where the patch sits tells you which part of the world the cell reports.

27
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.400
How plus and minus are laid out tells you which stimulus it reports best.

28
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:53.573
Cells of the thalamic relay, the lateral geniculate nucleus have fields of the same

29
00:01:53.573 --> 00:01:54.573
kind.

30
00:01:54.966 --> 00:02:00.026
It is a good first approximation for many cells in the retina and thalamus, and for

31
00:02:00.026 --> 00:02:01.386
simple cells in cortex.

32
00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:03.686
Later we find where it stops.

33
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:11.130
Keep the rule. Add up the light in the patch, plus on ON regions and minus on OFF

34
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:11.470
regions.
